// RUNTIME_ABORT_EXPECTED
// EXPECT-RUNTIME: String index out of bounds
// FDN-004: the write side (`s[i] = c;`, ArrayAccessExprAST::codegenLValue())
// does NOT check the `strlen()` upper bound a read gets, because the write
// target is very often a `gc_alloc`'d buffer being filled one byte at a time
// BEFORE its NUL terminator is written (every strings.tr function that builds
// a string this way would panic on its own very first write otherwise -- see
// EmitStringBoundsCheck()'s doc comment, src/codegen/CodegenArray.cpp, for the
// confirmed-live failure this test's positive counterpart would be).
//
// #256: a negative index is now counted from the end, so the write is bad only
// when it reaches past the start. A zero-filled buffer has strlen 0, so even
// `buf[-1]` wraps to -1 and still panics -- which is what this pins.
//
// The buffer is `gc_alloc_zeroed`, not `gc_alloc`: a negative index makes the
// write path call `strlen()` on the buffer, and `gc_alloc` is plain malloc, so
// its first bytes are whatever the heap held. This test used to rely on that
// being zero; AddressSanitizer (the gc_hardening_asan CI job) caught the
// `strlen` reading 5 bytes past a 4-byte block.
fn main(): int {
    unsafe {
        mut buf: string = gc_alloc_zeroed(4) to string;
        immut i: int = 0 - 1;
        buf[i] = 'x';
    }
    return 0;
}
